  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 104 AM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nAs of 1 AM CDT, temperatures around the area are in the 50s to low \n60s with winds out of the east to southeast at 10 mph or less. The \nmain question for today's forecast will be when and where drizzle \nturns into rain. Low stratus/fog will continue through today and\nmuch of Saturday.\n\nA shortwave will make its way into the region today moving generally \nwest to east and this will bring the forcing that transitions us \nfrom drizzle to rain. Looking at model soundings, the NAM brings \nthings in around noon for areas west of the James River. This is \nfaster than the RAP and HRRR, and more moist than the GFS. These \nother models bring the rain in this evening. Precipitation will move \ninto south central SD, moving west and north into north central SD \nbefore moving back east to cover eastern SD. Either scenario has \nprecipitation lasting through Saturday morning, moving east and out \nof the area during the afternoon. Chance of more than a quarter of \nan inch is 50-60% east of the James River, between 30 and 50% west \nof the James River to a line between Eureka and Chamberlain, and \nless than 20% across central SD. Our next chance of rain will be \nlate Sunday night/early Monday morning through at least Tuesday \nevening as a low level low moves across the ND/SD border. \nPrecipitation amounts with this event are quite a bit higher. Chance \nof more than half an inch in 24 hours between Monday and Tuesday \nmornings is 50-70% between the Missouri and Prairie Coteau/Sisseton \nHills.\n\nTemperatures through the weekend will be right around to slightly \nabove average before falling back to slightly below average for next \nwork week. Winds this afternoon across north central SD will gust up \nto around 30 mph.",
